diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — host_1_1_1_1 being unable to ping any host in the
10.200.0.0/24 server farm — does not exist in the live lab. Every component
of the forwarding path was tested and found fully operational.

host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2/24) has a correct default gateway of 10.1.1.1
(switch_dist_1_1's br0). switch_dist_1_1 has an active OSPF Full adjacency
with router_core_1 and carries the correct inter-area OSPF route for
10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 (router_core_1). router_core_1 holds a Full
adjacency with both router_core_3 and switch_dist_1_1, and forwards
traffic for 10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.3 (router_core_3:eth0). router_core_3
forwards to switch_server_access over the 172.16.0.10/31 link, where
switch_server_access has 10.200.0.0/24 directly connected on br0 (10.200.0.1)
and an OSPF Full adjacency back to router_core_3. All four web servers
(10.200.0.3–.6) and the DNS server (10.200.0.2) responded to pings from
host_1_1_1_1 with 0% packet loss, and an HTTP GET to web_server_0 returned
the expected page. Interface error counters are zero on all transit devices,
no iptables or traffic-control rules exist anywhere in the path, and the OSPF
LSDB is complete with all required type-1, type-2, and type-3 LSAs.

Root cause: There is no fault. The network is healthy and the helpdesk ticket
is a false premise. No corrective action is needed.
